      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Both SGLT2 inhibitors and GLP-1 receptor agonists are recommended by the American Diabetes Association (ADA) as preferred first-line or early treatment options for type 2 diabetes\u2014particularly for patients with or at high risk for cardiovascular disease, heart failure , or chronic kidney disease.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Both SGLT2 inhibitors and GLP-1 receptor agonists are recommended by the American Diabetes Association (ADA) as preferred first-line or early treatment options for type 2 diabetes"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Clinical Differences"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Primary Mechanism & Delivery:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "SGLT2 inhibitors (e.g., empagliflozin, dapagliflozin) are oral medications that work by helping the kidneys remove excess glucose through urine."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "GLP-1 receptor agonists (e.g., semaglutide, dulaglutide) are typically injectable (though oral semaglutide exists) that mimic natural gut hormones to stimulate insulin release, slow digestion, and curb appetite .",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  1,
                  2,
                  3
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              2,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Core Strengths & Prioritization:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Choose SGLT2 inhibitors if the patient has a primary history or high risk of heart failure or chronic kidney disease, as they significantly reduce heart failure hospitalizations and slow kidney decline."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Choose GLP-1 receptor agonists if the primary goals include superior weight loss , greater HbA1c reduction, or robust reduction of major adverse cardiovascular events (MACE) like stroke and heart attack.",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  1,
                  2,
                  4,
                  5
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              2,
              4,
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Side Effects & Contraindications:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "SGLT2 inhibitors: Higher risk of genital fungal infections, urinary tract infections, and diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA); require a specific kidney function threshold (eGFR > 60 ml/min/1.73m\u00b2 for optimal glucose-lowering initiation)."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "GLP-1 receptor agonists: Common gastrointestinal side effects (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea); contraindicated in individuals with a personal or family history of medullary thyroid carcinoma or Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia type 2 (MEN2).",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  2,
                  6,
                  7
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              6,
              7
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
